Ported and inspired parts
-------------------------

* main/families/c5_c6_c61/dc_cal.h is a port of receiver_dc_calibrate() from
  main/targets/esp32s31/streaming/dc.c of ESP-SDR (ESPARGOS,
  GPL-3.0-or-later) to the ESP32-C5.

* The gap-free PARLIO capture path (main/families/c5_c6_c61/parlio_cap.h:
  routing the modem diagnostic signals to pads via the GPIO matrix and reading
  them back with PARLIO RX, muting the Wi-Fi promiscuous filter during a run)
  and the TUNEM 5 call sequence in main/targets/esp32c5/tuning.h follow ideas
  of the C5VRX project by konradit, which is licensed GPL-3.0-only. These
  parts are an independent implementation; no C5VRX code was copied.
  Should any part nevertheless be considered derived from C5VRX, that part is
  covered by GPL-3.0-only. GPL-3.0-only and GPL-3.0-or-later code can be
  combined; the combined work is then distributed under the terms of the GNU
  GPL version 3. ESPsoup binary releases are therefore distributed under
  GPL-3.0.

Third-party components
----------------------

Third-party components keep their own licenses and copyright notices, in
particular the Apache-2.0 ESP-IDF compatibility code in
platform/esp32s2/esp_usb_cdc_rom_console/, the ESP-DSP component in
components/esp-dsp (Apache-2.0, see components/esp-dsp/LICENSE) and the
FFT kernels derived from ESP-DSP in main/targets/esp32s3/s3_fft_rnd.S and
main/targets/esp32s31/burst/s31_fft_rnd.S. Binary builds also contain
ESP-IDF including Espressif's binary Wi-Fi/PHY libraries (Apache-2.0).

Source code for binaries
------------------------

Whoever distributes ESPsoup firmware binaries must also make the
corresponding source code available under the GPL (GPLv3 section 6), for
example the tagged commit of this repository that the binary was built from
and the pinned ESP-IDF revision listed in firmware-targets.json.

